  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Combating Wildlife Issues in Belize to address illegal wildlife crime, to improve wildlife.security.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project will improve wildlife security throughout Belize by strengthening national wildlife law enforcement and launching a national public campaign to fight wildlife crime. The project is intended to conserve priority wildlife species including the Central American river turtle, yellow headed parrot, great curassow, white lipped peccary, scarlet macaw, Geoffroys spider monkey, Yucatan black howler monkey and jaguar by reducing illegal and unsustainable harvesting of wildlife and illegal wildlife trade. Specific activities include: 1) revision and dissemination of a National Threatened Species List; 2) creation of a National Wildlife Law Enforcement Network across multiple enforcement agencies to respond to wildlife crimes; 3) training 20 wildlife law enforcement officers of the Forest Department and NGO protected area co-managers as well as 50 multi-agency enforcement personnel in wildlife enforcement; 4) adoption of a National Wildlife Awareness Strategy and associated communication plan; and 5) implementation of a public campaign on wildlife crime including outreach at 12 national and regional events, 40 schools within high wildlife crime communities, targeted engagement of hunting communities, dealers and restaurants, and media packages for radio broadcasts.</narrative>
  </description>
